{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-19913fb7bbde415ea0a51cceba519c97","ordinal":81,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.81","text":"\n\nWe differ in idiosyncrasies in that some people digest beef more easily than little rock-fish, and are given a violent stomach upset by a drop of weak Lesbian wine. There was, they say, an old woman in Attica who took thirty drachms of hemlock without danger, and Lysis took four drachms of opium without ill effect."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-650450b8bc1843b08b6cd91f7c72cb6c","ordinal":82,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.82","text":"\n\nDemophon, Alexander’s table-servant, shivered in the sun or in a bath and felt warm in the shade. Athenagoras of Argos could be stung by scorpions and venomous spiders without ill effect. The people called the Psylli are not harmed even when bitten by snakes or asps,\n〔1〕 The Psylli were a North African people famed as snake-charmers immune to snakebite."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e9a733c9b09e4b3b99ba5daab3e600d4","ordinal":83,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.83","text":"\n\nand the Tentyrites in Egypt are not harmed by crocodiles. Again, those Ethiopians who live across from Meroë along the river Astapus eat scorpions, snakes and the like without danger. And Rufinus of Chalcis drank hellebore without vomiting or being purged at all: he took and digested it like something he was used to.\n〔1〕 Hellebore was the standard ancient emetic and purgative."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6cb55854042947468ce3df21fb976293","ordinal":84,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.84","text":"\n\nChrysermus the Herophilean, whenever he took pepper, was in danger of a heart attack. Soterichus the surgeon was seized with a violent stomach upset whenever he smelled catfish cooking. Andron of Argos was so free from thirst that he even traveled through waterless Libya without needing a drink. Tiberius Caesar could see in the dark. Aristotle tells of a man from Thasos who thought that the image of a man was always walking ahead of him.\n〔1〕 A Herophilean was a follower of Herophilus, the great Alexandrian physician of the third century BC."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d4141d531796426497603671930cdfca","ordinal":85,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.85","text":"\n\nSince there is so much variation among human beings in their bodies—to be content with mentioning a few of the many cases recorded by the dogmatists—it is likely that human beings also differ from one another in the soul itself. For the body is a kind of imprint of the soul, as the wisdom of physiognomy shows. But the greatest indication of the great, indeed endless, difference in human intellect is the disagreement in what the dogmatists say, both about other things and about what one should choose and what one should avoid."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-92bfd4f6e8d94ec3a21e17ffefe65894","ordinal":86,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.86","text":"\n\nThe poets, then, have aptly expressed themselves on these matters. Pindar says: “One man is gladdened by storm-footed horses,\ntheir honors ⟨and⟩ crowns;\nothers by a life in chambers rich with gold;\nand someone delights in crossing the swell of the sea\nin a swift ship.” And the poet says: “Different men delight in different deeds.” Tragedy too is full of such remarks. It says, for instance: “If the same thing were fine and wise for everyone by nature,\nthere would be no disputed strife among mankind”; and again: “Strange, that the same thing should please some mortals\nand be hateful to others.” Since, then, choice and avoidance lie in pleasure\n〔1〕 “The poet” is Homer (Odyssey 14.228); the first tragic passage is from Euripides, Phoenician Women 499–500, the second from an unidentified play."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-315a7a7a484f4fa793951c988a17e07e","ordinal":87,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.87","text":"\n\nand displeasure, and pleasure and displeasure lie in perception and impression, then when some people choose the very things others avoid, it follows that we should infer that they are not moved alike by the same things either; otherwise they would have chosen or avoided the same things alike. And if ⟨the same⟩ things move people differently owing to the differences among them, suspension of judgment would reasonably be brought in on this ground too. We can perhaps say how each of the underlying things appears relative to each difference, but we cannot declare what it is [potentially] in its nature."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ebaf56af9f4b4d16bdfb199e61e5f007","ordinal":88,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.88","text":"\n\nFor we will believe either all human beings or some. If all, we will be attempting the impossible and accepting opposites. If some, let them tell us which ones we should assent to. The Platonist will say Plato, the Epicurean Epicurus, and the rest likewise. And so, by quarreling without any way of deciding between them, they will bring us round once again to suspension of judgment."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7f9ded10e92e485dbdd6df12f33b6e15","ordinal":89,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.89","text":"\n\nAnyone who says we should assent to the majority will be making a childish suggestion. No one can go round all human beings and work out what the majority think. It is possible that among some peoples we know nothing of, things that are rare among us are true of most, while things that happen to most of us are rare there. Most of them, say, feel no pain when bitten by spiders, while a few occasionally do; and analogously with the other idiosyncrasies mentioned earlier. So suspension of judgment is necessarily brought in by the differences among human beings too."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9b15115d4c124d2bb1710a77a6318c49","ordinal":90,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.90","text":"\n\nThe dogmatists, being rather in love with themselves, say that they should be given preference over other human beings in judging things. We know that their claim is absurd. (They are themselves part of the disagreement; and if they give themselves preference and judge appearances on that basis, they take for granted the point under investigation before they have even begun to judge, by entrusting the judgment to themselves.)"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2ef532043fae4bb6987d84ebe59fe8fe","ordinal":91,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.91","text":"\n\nNevertheless, so that we may arrive at suspension of judgment even when we rest the argument on a single human being—for instance, the wise man of their dreams—we take up the mode that is third in order. This, we said, is the one from the differences among the senses. That the senses differ from one another is evident."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-aea270b62e70431ba850a2478a49ba56","ordinal":92,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.92","text":"\n\nPaintings, at any rate, seem to the eye to have recesses and projections, but not to touch. Honey appears pleasant to the tongue for some people, but unpleasant to the eyes; so it is impossible to say whether it is purely pleasant or unpleasant. Likewise with perfume: it delights the sense of smell but is unpleasant to the taste."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c653acf5b91d432d98b469181e63903e","ordinal":93,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.93","text":"\n\nAgain, spurge juice is painful to the eyes but painless to all the rest of the body; so we will not be able to say whether, as far as its own nature goes, it is purely painless to bodies or painful. Rainwater is beneficial to the eyes but roughens the windpipe and lungs—as olive oil does too, although it soothes the skin. And the electric ray, placed on the extremities, makes them numb, but can be placed on the rest of the body without harm. So we will not be able to say what each of these things is like in its nature, but it is possible to say what it appears like on each occasion."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a066ebc59c464157ade3745d4c672ebc","ordinal":94,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.94","text":"\n\nOne could say more than this; but so as not to linger, given the plan [of the mode] of this treatise, this is what needs to be said. Each of the perceptible things that appear to us seems to strike us as many-sided—an apple, for example, as smooth, fragrant, sweet and yellow. So it is non-evident whether it really has only these qualities; or whether it has just one quality but appears different because of the different constitutions of the sense organs; or whether it has more qualities than appear, some of which do not strike us."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-59a13c71663c4ada9a8a028c0a3ef380","ordinal":95,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.95","text":"\n\nThat it has just one quality can be inferred from what we said earlier about food being distributed into bodies, about water being distributed into trees, and about breath ⟨blown⟩ into pipes, panpipes and similar instruments. For the apple, too, may be uniform and yet be observed as different according to the difference of the sense organs through which it is perceived."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f6e8be75ff9648d3b88d019247492355","ordinal":96,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.96","text":"\n\nThat the apple may have more qualities than the ones that appear to us we infer as follows. Let us imagine someone who from birth has touch, smell and taste, but can neither hear nor see. He will suppose that there is nothing visible or audible at all, and that only those three kinds of quality exist that he is able to perceive."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-23a724ef1f4c425c87d1462736aad319","ordinal":97,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.97","text":"\n\nSo it is possible that we too, having only the five senses, perceive only those of the apple’s qualities that we are capable of perceiving. Other qualities may exist, striking other sense organs that we have no share in; and that is why we do not perceive the perceptible things that correspond to them either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-901d0ba6eb2e42658114175e3814c713","ordinal":98,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.98","text":"\n\n“But nature,” someone will say, “made the senses commensurate with their objects.” What nature? There is so much undecidable disagreement among the dogmatists about whether nature even exists. Whoever is to decide this very question—whether nature exists—will, if he is an ordinary person, be unconvincing by their standards; and if he is a philosopher, he will be part of the disagreement, and himself under judgment rather than a judge."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-da6bd127495140ba8bc001aca9bad1b6","ordinal":99,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.99","text":"\n\nNow, ⟨if⟩ it is possible that the apple has only the qualities we seem to perceive, or more than these, or again not even those that strike us, then what the apple is like will be non-evident to us. The same argument holds for the other objects of perception. And if the senses do not apprehend external things, neither can the intellect apprehend them, ⟨since its guides lead it astray⟩. So on this argument too, suspension of judgment about external objects will seem to follow."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4f64d8c1ddb74d0c9464b26f0b4c8739","ordinal":100,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.100","text":"\n\nSo that we may also end up in suspension of judgment when we rest the argument on each single sense, or even set the senses aside altogether, we adopt the fourth mode of suspension as well. This is the one said to depend on circumstances, by “circumstances” meaning conditions. We say it is observed in being in a natural or an unnatural ⟨state⟩, in being awake or asleep, in age, in moving or being at rest, in hating or loving, in being in need or sated, in being drunk or sober, in prior conditions, in being confident or afraid, [or] in being distressed or glad."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-784e109c4a794915bac0e6b9b2f23149","ordinal":101,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.101","text":"\n\nFor instance, things strike people as dissimilar according as they are in a natural or an unnatural state. People who are delirious or possessed by a god think they hear spirits, while we do not. Likewise they often say they perceive a waft of storax or frankincense or something of the sort, and many other things, while we perceive nothing. The same water poured on inflamed areas seems to them scalding, but to us lukewarm. The same cloak appears orange to people with bloodshot eyes, but not to me. The same honey appears sweet to me but bitter to people with jaundice."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-de5de4f039554968ae408a4226d0af3a","ordinal":102,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.102","text":"\n\nSomeone may say that it is an intermixture of certain humors that produces unsuitable impressions from the underlying things in people who are in an unnatural state. We must reply that healthy people too have humors mixed in them; so these humors could be making external objects—which in their nature are such as they appear to those said to be in an unnatural state—appear otherwise to the healthy."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-21ad02d83dcd4b099fe3462723b65dda","ordinal":103,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.103","text":"\n\nTo grant those humors the power to alter the underlying things, but not these, is a fabrication. For just as the healthy are in a state natural to the healthy but unnatural to the sick, so too the sick are in a state unnatural to the healthy but natural to the sick. So the sick too should be believed, since they too are in a natural state—relative to something."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3f1325ebd1964e0985acfc7537c13565","ordinal":104,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.104","text":"\n\nDifferent impressions arise according as we are asleep or awake. The way we have impressions in sleep is not the way we have them awake, and the way we have them awake is not the way we have them in sleep. So their existence or non-existence is not absolute but relative—relative to sleeping or to waking. It is reasonable, then, that in sleep we see things which do not exist in the waking state, though they are not absolutely non-existent: they exist in sleep, just as the things of waking life exist even though they do not exist in sleep."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ba57d71ae99049548ddf04c3a54f1960","ordinal":105,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.105","text":"\n\nAccording to age: the same air seems cold to the old but mild to those in their prime; the same color appears faint to older people but vivid to those in their prime; and likewise the same sound seems faint to some but clearly audible to others."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e7d9245eb1304c0c97f7bb6b5da83e55","ordinal":106,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.106","text":"\n\nPeople who differ in age are also moved dissimilarly in what they choose and avoid. Children, for instance, take balls and hoops very seriously; people in their prime choose other things, and the old still others. From this we conclude that the same underlying things produce different impressions according to differences of age too."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4fc56f36d3c7499a83755821a4b61eba","ordinal":107,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.107","text":"\n\nThings appear dissimilar according as we are moving or at rest. Things we see as motionless when we are standing still seem to be moving when we sail past them."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5a1bff4d61cc4246a9f1b43b63f5bee7","ordinal":108,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.108","text":"\n\nAccording to loving or hating: some people are utterly revolted by pork, while others eat it with the greatest pleasure. That is why Menander said: “And what does he look like, even in his face,\nsince he became like this? Like a wild beast!\nDoing no wrong makes us beautiful as well.” And many men whose beloveds are ugly think them extremely beautiful."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ebf60787d62149c3b15276f0899f9393","ordinal":109,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.109","text":"\n\nAccording to being hungry or sated: the same dish seems very pleasant to the hungry but unpleasant to the sated. According to being drunk or sober: things we think shameful when sober don’t appear shameful to us when we’re drunk."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4ff4a28014f9466cb8065395ecf8de48","ordinal":110,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.110","text":"\n\nAccording to prior conditions: the same wine appears sour to people who have just eaten dates or dried figs, but seems sweet to people who have had nuts or chickpeas. And the vestibule of the bathhouse warms those coming in from outside but chills those going out, if they linger in it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a59d5b6d1a3c4ba3b7565495ec8aeaaf","ordinal":111,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.111","text":"\n\nAccording to being afraid or confident: the same thing seems fearful and terrible to the coward, but not at all to someone bolder. According to being distressed or glad: the same things are burdensome to the distressed but pleasant to the glad."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c2bcd7122cd640bb95f738d57ef0cca6","ordinal":112,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.112","text":"\n\nThere is this much variation depending on conditions, and people are in different conditions at different times. So it is perhaps easy to say what each underlying object appears like to each person, but not what it is like, since the variation is also undecidable. For whoever decides it is either in some of the conditions just mentioned or in no condition at all. Now, to say that he is in no condition whatsoever—that he is neither healthy nor ill, neither moving nor at rest, of no particular age, and free of the other conditions as well—is utterly absurd. But if he judges the impressions while he is in some condition, he will be a party to the disagreement,"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e68c8456405d4a009b8ed73b3338abdc","ordinal":113,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.113","text":"\n\nand besides, he will not be an impartial judge of external objects, because he is muddied by the conditions he is in. So the waking person cannot compare the impressions of sleepers with those of people awake, nor the healthy person the impressions of the sick with those of the healthy. For we assent to what is present and affecting us at present rather than to what is not present."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-48713689b4214e8db0eb953abbc7a61c","ordinal":114,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.114","text":"\n\nThe variation in such impressions is undecidable for another reason too. Whoever prefers one impression to another, and one circumstance to another, does so either without judging and without proof, or by judging and proving. But he can do it neither without these (for then he will not be credible) nor with them. For if he judges the impressions, he will certainly judge them by a criterion."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-28cc64edc71f438092a3eb1435009c73","ordinal":115,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.115","text":"\n\nNow he will say that this criterion is either true or false. If false, he will not be credible. If he says it is true, he will say that the criterion is true either without proof or with proof. If without proof, he will not be credible. If with proof, the proof too will certainly have to be true, or else he will not be credible. So when he says that the proof he has adopted to confirm the criterion is true, will he have judged it or not?"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6fd84f8fa98449e5843298a7d6e974e0","ordinal":116,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.116","text":"\n\nIf he has not judged it, he will not be credible. If he has, he will obviously say that he judged it by a criterion—and for that criterion we will ask for a proof, and for that proof a criterion. For a proof always needs a criterion in order to be confirmed, and a criterion always needs a proof in order to be shown true. A proof cannot be sound unless a true criterion is already there, and a criterion cannot be true unless the proof has already been confirmed."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d645522e76b649b7af321cf256f89c3b","ordinal":117,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.117","text":"\n\nAnd so the criterion and the proof fall into the mode of reciprocity, in which both are found to lack credibility: each waits on the other for its credibility, and so is just as uncredible as the other. If, then, no one can prefer one impression to another either without proof ⟨and criterion⟩ or with them, the different impressions that arise from different conditions will be undecidable. So, as far as this mode goes too, suspension of judgment about the nature of external objects is brought in.\n〔1〕 The mode of reciprocity is circular reasoning: A is established by B, and B in turn by A. It returns as the fifth of the Five Modes (1.169)."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-dd2042c8835746f5a83eb651ddfde180","ordinal":118,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.118","text":"\n\nThe fifth argument is the one depending on positions, distances and places. For depending on each of these, the same things appear different. The same colonnade, for example, seen from one end, appears to taper, but seen from the middle it appears symmetrical on all sides. The same ship appears small and stationary from far off, but large and moving close up. And the same tower appears round from far off but square close up."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3ff2c99559c3494a9f5a23dc2d9bed90","ordinal":119,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.119","text":"\n\nThese depend on distances. Depending on places: lamplight appears dim in the sunlight but bright in the dark; the same oar appears bent in the water but straight out of it; an egg is soft inside the bird but hard in the air; lyngurium is liquid inside the lynx but hard in the air; coral is soft in the sea but hard in the air; and a sound appears one way when it is made in a panpipe, another in a reed pipe, and another simply in the air.\n〔1〕 Lyngurium (“lynx-stone”) was believed to be the lynx’s urine, which hardened into a gem on contact with the air."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-487c422490574b3084f6d4533a20b2b2","ordinal":120,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.120","text":"\n\nDepending on positions: the same painting appears smooth when tilted back, but tilted forward to a certain degree it seems to have recesses and projections. And the necks of doves appear different in color depending on the different angles at which they are turned."}]}